<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Merchandising.ClientCommon - API client, token/session handling, common
    controls, converters, styles, validation helpers, and connection-state
    behaviour. Spec section 7.

    ====================================================================
    REFERENCES Merchandising.Contracts AND NOTHING ELSE.
    ====================================================================

    Never the server-side infrastructure library, and never a database
    connector package of any kind. That single rule is what mechanically
    guarantees a database credential cannot reach a client laptop - it is
    enforced by guardrail G-B, not by good intentions. The forbidden names
    are listed in scripts/check-no-csharp.ps1 and are deliberately NOT
    repeated here: G-B matches raw file text, so writing them out even in
    a comment fails the build. See CLAUDE.md section 4.

    On the target framework: this is $(MerchWindowsTfm) with UseWPF, not
    $(MerchNetTfm), because spec section 7 puts "common controls,
    converters, styles" and "XAML resources" in this project. A plain
    net10.0 library cannot hold a ResourceDictionary or implement
    System.Windows.Data.IValueConverter. P1-01's card said "libraries use
    $(MerchNetTfm)"; the spec outranks the card (CLAUDE.md section 1) and
    the alternative was a twelfth project split out later for no gain.
    Referencing the net10.0 Contracts project from here is fine.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Merchandising.ClientCommon</RootNamespace>
    <TargetFramework>$(MerchWindowsTfm)</TargetFramework>
    <UseWPF>true</UseWPF>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Merchandising.Contracts\Merchandising.Contracts.vbproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
